# Transcendence of the q-expansion j(q) over ℚ

Theorem statement: `ModularCurve.transcendental_jq` (`39f04249-53e5-5935-83cd-a208f4acfafa`), by
Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `3941aa83-1eb9-56a4-b9b3-5023ee22452b`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

The statement concerns the element `jq` of the field of formal Laurent series $\mathbb{Q}((q))$,
realised as the Hahn series field `LaurentSeries ℚ` over the value group $\mathbb{Z}$: by definition
`jq` is the product of the Hahn series `HahnSeries.single (-1) 1`, i.e. the monomial $q^{-1}$, with
the image under `HahnSeries.ofPowerSeries` of `jNumQ`, the power series obtained from the integral
power series `jNum` by applying the coefficientwise ring homomorphism $\mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{Q}$.
Thus `jq` is $q^{-1}$ times a power series with rational (indeed integral) coefficients, the
$q$-expansion of the modular invariant. There are no variables and no hypotheses. The conclusion is
that `jq` is transcendental over $\mathbb{Q}$ with respect to the $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra structure on
`LaurentSeries ℚ`: `jq` is not algebraic over $\mathbb{Q}$, equivalently no nonzero polynomial in
$\mathbb{Q}[X]$ annihilates it under evaluation at `jq`. In particular the $\mathbb{Q}$-subalgebra
generated by `jq` inside $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ is a polynomial ring, and the subfield it generates is a
rational function field in one variable.

This is the transcendence fact underlying the identification of $\mathbb{Q}(j(q)) \subseteq
\mathbb{Q}((q))$ with the function field of $X(1) \cong \mathbb{P}^1_{\mathbb{Q}}$, and hence the
starting point for the description of the function fields $\mathbb{Q}(j(q), j(q^N))$ attached to
$X_0(N)$. It is used throughout the curve layer of the algebraic $X_0(N)$ development, for instance
in the constructions of fibre models and charts at cusps and in the finiteness and degree
computations for the associated models.

